    <!-- Under the palette choice: what the selected one is good and bad at. -->
    <string name="palette_hint_grayscale">Grayscale: shades without colour. Best for reading temperatures and for long sessions; a small hot spot is easy to miss.</string>
    <string name="palette_hint_iron">Iron: black through red to white, like heated metal. Hot spots stand out, so it suits wiring and overheating; weak in the cold end.</string>
    <string name="palette_hint_rainbow">Rainbow: the most colours per degree. Shows small differences and gradients, but draws edges that are not really there.</string>
    <string name="palette_hint_arctic">Arctic: most of the scale in blues. Made for cold spots, draughts and thermal bridges in a building; poor for hot objects.</string>
    <string name="palette_hint_lava">Lava: black to white through the reds. Hot areas read at a glance (pipes, heaters, stoves); little contrast in the cold end.</string>
